375/assets/b6fe02d5b3d96aeaa6a4edc9a027ec69993330252322e10a57618dd499bb02b5.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb2aa274fc22a823f7da17bbc9877c568f92c7bf9552f6f68f62869d3e322f1d"} {"question" "q-162150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/assets/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb2aa274fc22a823f7da17bbc9877c568f92c7bf9552f6f68f62869d3e322f1d"} {"question" "q-162150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb2aa274fc22a823f7da17bbc9877c568f92c7bf9552f6f68f62869d3e322f1d"} {"question" "q-162150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6293cc96a6d4aa4d9688bc8a6add7d10e203b0bfe03b78c08ef4c2c0c4a8f0f8"} {"question" "q-162150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb2aa274fc22a823f7da17bbc9877c568f92c7bf9552f6f68f62869d3e322f1d"} {"question" "q-162150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A line segment"] ["text" "A point, different from the origin"] ["text" "A rectangle"] ["text" "A triangle"] ["text" "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb2aa274fc22a823f7da17bbc9877c568f92c7bf9552f6f68f62869d3e322f1d"} {"question" "q-162150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb2aa274fc22a823f7da17bbc9877c568f92c7bf9552f6f68f62869d3e322f1d"} {"question" "q-162204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/assets/2cb5cf6d3307c1291dbbb785cd71ed6edecba3d448afbc2f5f5c2d1128b457c1.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb2aa274fc22a823f7da17bbc9877c568f92c7bf9552f6f68f62869d3e322f1d"} {"question" "q-162204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 6-(-4)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the quadrilateral under the action of $T$ is a line segment." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb2aa274fc22a823f7da17bbc9877c568f92c7bf9552f6f68f62869d3e322f1d"} {"question" "q-162204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6293cc96a6d4aa4d9688bc8a6add7d10e203b0bfe03b78c08ef4c2c0c4a8f0f8"} {"question" "q-162204" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb2aa274fc22a823f7da17bbc9877c568f92c7bf9552f6f68f62869d3e322f1d"} {"question" "q-162204" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A line segment"] ["text" "A point, different from the origin"] ["text" "A quadrilateral"] ["text" "A triangle"] ["text" "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb2aa274fc22a823f7da17bbc9877c568f92c7bf9552f6f68f62869d3e322f1d"} {"question" "q-162204" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A line segment"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/state.json" "file_sha256" "eb2aa274fc22a823f7da17bbc9877c568f92c7bf9552f6f68f62869d3e322f1d"}] "usage" {"q-96873" [] "q-162150" []} "basis" 1927 "retractions" [[17592186106472 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/q-96873.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T$?"] [17592186105993 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186102206 17592186102207 17592186102208 17592186102209 17592186102210 17592186